d to a power:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{81} & =\\frac{1\\times 1}{9\\times 9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\times \\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{9})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the reciprocal of the base $(\\frac{1}{9}),$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the *opposite* sign $(-2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{9})}^{2}={9}^{-2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489244 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176092 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8987f52a-5da7-51db-8266-9abc33d3f326" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Fraction as a Power With a Negative Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d8803b9-9eda-553f-a752-47d73f4bbb07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{4}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488622} {:answer/id #uuid "4dc9514e-15da-5744-b3cb-398a119df9ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{4}^{-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488623} {:answer/id #uuid "7227bf75-2f02-5620-88b4-37a4f6a8cfad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{4}^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488699} {:answer/id #uuid "e75f783f-03f5-5e7e-8e42-cbec60546365" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{3}^{-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488953} {:answer/id #uuid "fd3e80ba-adf6-5413-b1da-31afe0a77c7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{4}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489008}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7227bf75-2f02-5620-88b4-37a4f6a8cfad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{4}^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488699} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5943d0a-4750-5c6b-97ba-3430e1cd7e36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489118}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "955d0666-e318-54f6-bac2-1bee61b1768a" :question/math-academy-id "q-6194" :question/problem "Express $\\frac{1}{64}$ as a base raised to a negative exponent." :question/worked-solution "First, we write $\\frac{1}{64}$ as a power with a negative exponent.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{64} & =\\frac{1\\times 1\\times 1}{4\\times 4\\times 4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\times \\frac{1}{4}\\times \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the reciprocal of the base $(\\frac{1}{4}),$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the *opposite* sign $(-3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3}={4}^{-3}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489296 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176091 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "47675504-c040-5b23-8ef1-73412a6b6f81" :knowledge-point/title "Raising a Fraction to a Negative Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01c88b39-c18f-5609-af42-c6464e1f7b4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488470} {:answer/id #uuid "5562f759-bcb2-503a-ac9b-ecb74fcf42ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488633} {:answer/id #uuid "8e1ae6d0-4bff-533a-a95d-92a0c5ae1908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488754} {:answer/id #uuid "c22dc3fd-3ecc-5018-99e3-cdf1d5738a25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488863} {:answer/id #uuid "c2ac44af-4e14-559a-9ea7-7a5197b44ac9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488864}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2ac44af-4e14-559a-9ea7-7a5197b44ac9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488864} :answer-field/id #uuid "3520d9ec-94dd-56e9-883d-3d54c63b1325" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489045}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fe935012-f0c6-58bc-9344-9d20da9c881b" :question/math-academy-id "q-71764" :question/problem "${(\\frac{3}{4})}^{-2}=$" :question/worked-solution "We find the reciprocal of the base $(\\frac{3}{4})$, and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the *opposite* sign $(+2)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{-2} & ={(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
